{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of upward momentum: When history shows a sharp price spike (e.g., >3 EUR in 2 days) and sentiment is positive (>=0.25), the model's flat or slightly declining forecast must be adjusted upward by at least 5-8 EUR to capture the continuation of the rally.",
    "Apply aggressive mean reversion after peaks: Following a price peak (e.g., >76 EUR), the model fails to anticipate the subsequent rapid decline. Reduce forecasts by 10-12 EUR over the 10-day horizon to align with the observed drop to ~63-65 EUR.",
    "Ignore sentiment lag in volatile regimes: Positive sentiment signals (1.00) often precede immediate price surges that the model misses entirely. Do not rely on sentiment history for smoothing; instead, treat positive sentiment spikes as triggers for immediate upward forecast revisions.",
    "Adjust for horizon-specific decay: The model assumes a stable mean (~69-71 EUR) regardless of recent volatility. In post-spike environments, forecasts must be dynamically lowered to reflect the new lower equilibrium (~67-68 EUR) rather than reverting to the historical average.",
    "Detect 'flat-line' bias: The model consistently outputs near-constant values regardless of the 18-day history trend. Override this by calculating the slope of the last 5 days and applying a momentum factor of 1.5x to the forecast trajectory."
  ]
}
